-- Float: abstract spec for the built-in floating-point sort.
-- Spec satisfaction facts (PartialEq[Float], PartialOrd[Float], NonEq[Float],
-- Numeric[Float], Ring[Float] — IEEE Float is partial, so NonEq not Eq/Ord)
-- live in the per-language binding files (e.g. rustland/anthill-stl/anthill/float.anthill)
-- — see proposal 038.
sort anthill.prelude.Float
  import anthill.prelude.{Eq, NonEq, Ord, Numeric}
  import anthill.prelude.algebra.{Ring}
  -- WI-20260825-KD9SW: a WRITTEN name in a LAW or CONSTRAINT HEAD needs the import.
  import anthill.prelude.PartialEq.{neq}
  import anthill.prelude.Multiplicative.{mul}

  -- WI-876: `Float`'s comparison surface, declared here and backed by the host in
  -- `rustland/anthill-stl/anthill/float.anthill`'s `operation_map`, which names the
  -- IEEE functions. NO `compare`: `Float` provides `PartialOrd` and NOT `Ord`,
  -- because a `NaN` operand is UNORDERED — every comparison answers false, and there
  -- is no total `compare` for `PartialOrd`'s default derivation to bottom out in.
  -- `TotalFloat` is the lawful-key wrapper.
  --
  -- That "Float's order is IEEE" is now a FACT IN FLOAT'S OWN BINDING rather than a
  -- branch inside a shared spec-op builtin — the same registration that had to serve
  -- every carrier, and therefore had to test its operands to find out which one it
  -- was serving.
  --
  -- WI-881: `max`/`min` are the IEEE-754 `maxNum`/`minNum`, and they are declared
  -- HERE rather than inherited. `Ord` is where the library's other `max`/`min`
  -- live, `Float` does not provide it, and `PartialOrd` has none — so until this
  -- there was NO WAY TO TAKE THE MAXIMUM OF TWO FLOATS AT ALL. Nor would inheriting
  -- have been right: `Ord`'s derivation is `if gte(a, b) then a else b`, and with
  -- a NaN operand every comparison is false, so it answers `1.0` on `(nan, 1.0)` and
  -- `nan` on `(1.0, nan)` — not commutative. The IEEE pair ABSORBS NaN instead, which
  -- is the `max` a float programmer means.
  --
  -- WHAT IS STILL MISSING, so the next reader does not read more into this than it
  -- says: these are reachable only by NAMING `Float`. No spec `Float` provides declares
  -- `max`, so a GENERIC `f[T](a: T, b: T) requires PartialOrd[T]` still cannot take a
  -- maximum, where the same code over `Int64` reaches `Ord.max`. Closing that needs
  -- a spec that owns "has a max" independent of totality, or 058 §3.8's per-provision
  -- conditions so `Ord`'s derivation can be conditioned rather than inherited.
  operation {
    gt(a: Float, b: Float) -> Bool         -- >, false if either operand is NaN
    gte(a: Float, b: Float) -> Bool        -- >=, false if either operand is NaN
    lt(a: Float, b: Float) -> Bool         -- <, false if either operand is NaN
    lte(a: Float, b: Float) -> Bool        -- <=, false if either operand is NaN
    max(a: Float, b: Float) -> Float       -- IEEE maxNum: max(nan, x) = x
    min(a: Float, b: Float) -> Float       -- IEEE minNum: min(nan, x) = x
  }

  -- Operations beyond what Numeric provides.
  --
  -- WI-881: every one of these is BACKED BY A HOST FUNCTION, named per operation in
  -- `rustland/anthill-stl/anthill/float.anthill`'s `operation_map`. They are body-less
  -- for the same reason the comparisons above are — the implementation is the host
  -- artifact's `f64` intrinsic — and before that clause existed they were body-less
  -- and backed by NOTHING: 24 of this sort's 32 declared operations died
  -- `OperationBodyMissing` at eval on a program that loaded clean, because a host
  -- carrier is exempt from the load-time backing check wholesale (WI-880 narrows it).
  --
  -- `floor`/`ceil`/`round` are PARTIAL, and that is the one place the two carriers
  -- stop lining up: `Float` has NaN, ±Infinity and a range far past `Int64`, so
  -- `floor(nan)` and `floor(1e300)` have no answer. The host raises rather than
  -- saturating silently; giving them a guarded `Error` effect row, which is what would
  -- say so in the SIGNATURE, is WI-882's shape.
  operation {
    abs(a: Float) -> Float                -- |a|; clears the sign bit (abs(-0.0) = 0.0)
    neg(a: Float) -> Float                -- -a; flips the sign bit (neg(0.0) = -0.0)
    div(a: Float, b: Float) -> Float      -- IEEE division; also `/` infix
    floor(a: Float) -> Int64                -- round toward negative infinity; partial
    ceil(a: Float) -> Int64                 -- round toward positive infinity; partial
    round(a: Float) -> Int64                -- round to nearest, half away from zero; partial
    recip(a: Float) -> Float              -- 1/a
    -- IEEE 754 predicates: needed because Float.eq returns false for NaN
    -- (NaN != NaN in IEEE), so users can't detect NaN via equality.
    isNaN(a: Float) -> Bool               -- true iff a is NaN
    isInfinite(a: Float) -> Bool          -- true iff a is +Inf or -Inf
    isFinite(a: Float) -> Bool            -- true iff a is neither NaN nor infinite

    -- Power / roots
    sqrt(a: Float) -> Float               -- principal square root, requires a >= 0
    hypot(a: Float, b: Float) -> Float    -- sqrt(a*a + b*b), avoids overflow
    fmod(a: Float, b: Float) -> Float     -- IEEE remainder (sign follows a)
    pow(base: Float, exp: Float) -> Float -- base ^ exp

    -- Trig (arguments in radians)
    sin(a: Float) -> Float
    cos(a: Float) -> Float
    tan(a: Float) -> Float
    asin(a: Float) -> Float               -- result in [-pi/2, pi/2]
    acos(a: Float) -> Float               -- result in [0, pi]
    atan(a: Float) -> Float               -- result in (-pi/2, pi/2)
    atan2(y: Float, x: Float) -> Float    -- 2-arg arctan, result in (-pi, pi]

    -- Exponential / logarithm
    exp(a: Float) -> Float                -- e^a
    log(a: Float) -> Float                -- natural log, requires a > 0
    log10(a: Float) -> Float              -- base-10 log, requires a > 0
    log2(a: Float) -> Float               -- base-2 log, requires a > 0

    -- Constants (nullary operations — invoked as `pi`, `e`, etc.)
    pi() -> Float                         -- π = 3.14159…
    e() -> Float                          -- e = 2.71828…
    tau() -> Float                        -- τ = 2π = 6.28318…
  }

  -- Special IEEE 754 values as term-level constants (WI-084 / proposal 039).
  -- Bodyless / host-supplied: anthill has no surface literal for ±Inf or NaN,
  -- so each is a `const` whose value is produced by a registered host builtin
  -- (anthill-core eval/builtins.rs). They give a name to the saturation bounds
  -- and the non-value — e.g. `Motor.set_position(infinity)` selects velocity
  -- mode, an unbounded range uses `negativeInfinity`/`infinity` as its ends.
  -- Detect them with the `isInfinite` / `isFinite` / `isNaN` predicates above
  -- (`nan` is unequal to everything, itself included, so `Eq` can't spot it).
  const infinity: Float                   -- +∞
  const negativeInfinity: Float           -- -∞
  const nan: Float                        -- IEEE NaN (the canonical quiet NaN)

  -- Laws and definitions.
  --
  -- WI-881 settles which of these four is WHICH, because the file used to say
  -- "-- Laws" over all of them and some were meant to be DEFINITIONS: a `[simp]`
  -- equation is executable — it inlines LHS→RHS and makes a body-less operation run —
  -- while an untagged rule is inert specification (a rule is not backing, WI-818), and
  -- the marker was simply forgotten here. `set.anthill`, `map.anthill` and
  -- `relation.anthill` all tag theirs and argue each one; this file did not.
  --
  -- THE DIVIDING LINE IS THE SIGN OF ZERO. IEEE `Float` distinguishes `+0.0` from
  -- `-0.0` (`recip` tells them apart: `+inf` vs `-inf`) while EVERY COMPARISON reads
  -- them equal. So a law that reconstructs a sign bit out of arithmetic or ordering
  -- is not the definition, however much it looks like one — which is what settles
  -- `neg` and `abs`. `tau`'s equation IS exact and is a law anyway, for the second
  -- reason: INLINING IS NOT DISPATCH, and it does not reach every call form.

  -- DEFINITION. `recip` IS `1/a`, bit for bit, for every input including ±0.0, ±inf
  -- and NaN — the host `f64::recip` is literally `1.0/self`. So the equation is the
  -- implementation and `recip` needs no host mapping of its own; `div` already has one.
  rule recip(?a) <=> div(1.0, ?a) [simp]

  -- SPECIFICATION, and an EXACT one — `2·π` is exact in binary floating point (doubling
  -- only increments the exponent), so this is the same f64 as the host's `TAU`. It
  -- WOULD have been a `[simp]` definition like `recip`'s, and it was until driving
  -- found what inlining cannot do: a `[simp]` head is an APPLICATION, so it matches
  -- `tau()` and NOT the BARE `tau` this sort's own comment advertises as a call form
  -- (a bare nullary call site is a `var_ref`). MEASURED — with `[simp]`, `pi` and `e`
  -- answered bare while `tau` alone still died `OperationBodyMissing`. Three constants
  -- of one family must behave alike, so `tau` is host-backed with its siblings and
  -- this stays a law. (Written with parentheses on both nullary names regardless: the
  -- bare spelling `rule tau <=> mul(2.0, pi)` matches nothing, which is how these four
  -- equations came to be inert in the first place. `map.anthill` records the
  -- mirror-image hazard on the RIGHT-hand side — `<=> none [simp]` parses as an
  -- instantiation `none[simp]`.)
  rule tau() <=> mul(2.0, pi())

  -- SPECIFICATION, restated. The old law was `neg(?a) <=> sub(0.0, ?a)`, which is
  -- FALSE: `neg(0.0)` is `-0.0` and `0.0 - 0.0` is `+0.0` (MEASURED — `recip` of the
  -- two answers `-inf` and `+inf`). Multiplication by `-1.0` flips the sign bit
  -- exactly, at zero and everywhere else, so this one is true; `neg` is still backed
  -- by the host intrinsic rather than by inlining it, because negating is one
  -- instruction and a `Numeric.mul` dispatch is not.
  rule neg(?a) <=> mul(-1.0, ?a)

  -- SPECIFICATION, restated. The old law was `abs(?a) <=> max(?a, neg(?a))`, and it
  -- was DOUBLY dead: it named `Ord.max`, which no `Float` value can reach
  -- (`Float` provides `PartialOrd`, not `Ord` — so `float.anthill` imported a name
  -- that could never be used), and `abs` is not definable by comparison at all. `abs`
  -- CLEARS the sign bit, `-0.0` and `+0.0` compare EQUAL, and the IEEE `max` this sort
  -- now declares is documented to return EITHER input when they compare equal — so the
  -- law is not merely wrong at `-0.0`, it is undefined there. The `ite(lt(?a, 0.0),
  -- neg(?a), ?a)` restatement the ticket floated fails on the same input. What is left
  -- is the true and useful part: `abs` erases whatever the sign was.
  rule abs(neg(?a)) <=> abs(?a)

  -- WI-658: Float provides NonEq (see the fact in the rust binding). The witness
  -- for "IEEE eq is not reflexive" is `nan` — `eq(nan, nan) = false`.
  rule nonEqRefl() <=> nan

  -- Constraints
  constraint recip_nonzero: neq(?a, 0.0) :- recip(?a)
  constraint div_float_nonzero: neq(?b, 0.0) :- div(?_, ?b)
  constraint sqrt_nonneg: gte(?a, 0.0) :- sqrt(?a)
  constraint log_positive: gt(?a, 0.0) :- log(?a)
  constraint log10_positive: gt(?a, 0.0) :- log10(?a)
  constraint log2_positive: gt(?a, 0.0) :- log2(?a)
end
